  "notes": "The man who put rock guitars and a rapped verse on country radio, and the owner of the bro-country drum grammar. The tempo band is unusually tight because the entry gives only one class: \"notated 78–95 with the backbeat on 2&4, equivalently 156–190 with the snare on beat 3\" — the folded 2&4 reading is encoded per the section's stated convention, so `halfTime` stays inherited false. The key set is the entry's headline: \"the most minor-key producer in this section — D♭ minor, C minor, F♯ minor, E♭ minor, A♭ minor and E minor all carry singles from his flagship act, against major-key cuts in G/A/D/F,\" with \"i–VI–III–VII, i–VII–VI, and I–♭VII–IV on the major rockers.\" The drum grammar is quoted whole: \"a slow heavy backbeat with kick on 1 and the 'and-of-2,' snare hard on the backbeat, 8th hats in verse opening to 16ths in chorus, programmed claps and a sampled 808-adjacent low kick layered under the acoustic kit, half-time chorus lifts, and a hard full-band drop-out before the last chorus.\" The 808 takes `counter_riff` because the entry names two layers with two jobs: \"overdriven live electric bass doubled by a short gated synth/sampled sub on the low-kick hits; on the country-rap cuts a longer sub with a slide into the downbeat.\" His most-copied structural invention is \"the rapped/spoken verse over a two-bar loop,\" which is the high melodic density ceiling and the `2_bars_per_chord` weighting. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium) — \"his own workflow statements are not reachable [LOW-CONF]\" and the catalogue readings carry the section's class mark. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 808 register, the clap offsets and the counter ratio.",
